# Rust
target/

# Fuzzing. The corpus is regenerable (`cargo run --example seed-corpus`) and CI
# caches its own, so committing it would only add churn. Crashes are not kept
# here either: anything found gets transcribed into REGRESSION_CORPUS in
# moqom-proto::testing, where it runs on stable CI instead of rotting in a
# directory nobody reads.
rs/fuzz/corpus/
rs/fuzz/artifacts/
rs/fuzz/coverage/
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb

# Go
go/bin/
*.test
*.out

# Node
node_modules/
dist/
.vite/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Apple
.DS_Store
xcuserdata/
*.xcuserstate
DerivedData/
*.xcworkspace/xcshareddata/IDEWorkspaceChecks.plist
Pods/

# SwiftPM build output. .build/workspace-state.json in particular looks like
# configuration and is not; it was committed once already.
.build/
.swiftpm/

# Every dependency is a local path today, so this resolves to nothing. Revisit
# if an external package is ever added: an app should commit it, a library
# should not.
Package.resolved

# Generated by xcodegen from project.yml. The pbxproj is thousands of generated
# UUIDs nobody can review, which is why project.yml is the source.
*.xcodeproj/

# The Team ID is not a secret but it identifies a person, and the account is
# personal for now. Signing.xcconfig carries the shared half and includes this
# optionally.
Local.xcconfig

# Terraform. State is in GCS and never on disk; a *.tfstate here means someone
# ran a root without initialising its backend, and it will contain credentials.
.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
crash.log
crash.*.log
*.tfvars
!*.tfvars.example
!*.tfvars.json.example
backend.hcl
!backend.hcl.example

# Root lock files are committed on purpose. They pin the exact provider builds
# and their checksums, so CI and a laptop resolve to the same code and a
# compromised registry release cannot be substituted silently. Regenerate with
# `terraform providers lock` for every platform that runs Terraform, not just
# your own — a lock file missing linux_amd64 breaks CI and looks like a network
# error when it does.
#
# Module lock files are ignored, and the asymmetry is deliberate. A module is
# never applied on its own; the only thing that initialises one is CI running
# `validate` against it. Such a run writes a lock file for whichever platform
# happened to produce it, and committing that single-platform file is strictly
# worse than having none: the next machine with a different OS fails init on a
# checksum mismatch. The pinning that matters already lives in the roots.
deploy/modules/*/.terraform.lock.hcl

# Secrets and local config
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.p8
*.p12
*.mobileprovision
service-account*.json

